{"title": "fis", "version": "v1", "page": 1, "body": "# 1. INTRODUCTION\n\nThe purpose of this paper is to present a novel approach to the problem of detecting and localizing anomalies in time series data. The proposed method is based on the use of a wavelet transform, which is a powerful tool for analyzing non-stationary signals. The wavelet transform decomposes a signal into different frequency components, allowing for the identification of anomalies by analyzing the distribution of energy across these components.\n\n## 1.1 Background\n\nTime series data are commonly used in various fields such as finance, engineering, and biology. Anomaly detection in time series data involves identifying unusual patterns or events that deviate from the expected behavior. Anomalies can be caused by various factors, including natural phenomena, human-made events, or system malfunctions.\n\nTraditionally, anomaly detection in time series data has been approached using statistical methods, such as the Z-score or the Kolmogorov-Smirnov test. These methods rely on assumptions about the underlying distribution of the data and may not be robust to noise or outliers. Wavelet transforms offer a more flexible and robust approach to anomaly detection, as they can capture both stationary and non-stationary features of the data.\n\n## 1.2 Problem Statement\n\nThe main problem addressed in this paper is the detection and localization of anomalies in time series data. Given a time series dataset, the goal is to identify segments of the data that exhibit abnormal behavior, such as sudden changes in amplitude or frequency. These anomalies can then be further investigated to determine their cause and potential impact.\n\n# 2. WAVELET TRANSFORM\n\n## 2.1 Definition\n\nA wavelet transform is a mathematical technique used to analyze signals that have been transformed into the time domain. It decomposes a signal into different frequency components, allowing for the identification of anomalies by analyzing the distribution of energy across these components.\n\n## 2.2 Key Concepts\n\n### 2.2.1 Wavelet Coefficients\n\nThe wavelet transform of a signal $x(t)$ at scale $s$ and location $t$ is given by:\n\n$$\nW_s(x)(t) = \\sum_{n=-\\infty}^{\\infty} x(n) \\psi_s(n - st)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\psi_s(n)$ is the wavelet function at scale $s$ and location $t$, and $x(n)$ is the original signal.\n\n### 2.2.2 Wavelet Transform Coefficients\n\nThe wavelet transform of a signal $x(t)$ at scale $s$ and location $t$ is given by:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{W}_s(x)(t) = \\sum_{n=-\\infty}^{\\infty} x(n) \\psi_s(n - st)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathcal{W}_s(x)(t)$ is the wavelet transform of $x(t)$ at scale $s$ and location $t$.\n\n## 2.3 Applications\n\nWavelet transforms have been applied in various fields, including image processing, speech recognition, and signal analysis. In time series data, wavelet transforms have been used to detect anomalies in stock market data, detect anomalies in sensor readings, and identify anomalies in financial time series data.\n\n# 3. METHODOLOGY\n\n## 3.1 Data Preprocessing\n\nThe first step in the methodology is to preprocess the time series data. This involves removing noise, outliers, and other artifacts that may affect the accuracy of the analysis. The data is also normalized to ensure that all values fall within a common range.\n\n## 3.2 Wavelet Transform\n\nThe second step is to apply the wavelet transform to the preprocessed data. The wavelet transform is performed at multiple scales and locations, allowing for the identification of anomalies at different levels of detail. The resulting coefficients are then used to construct a wavelet frame, which is a representation of the signal in the time domain.\n\n## 3.3 Anomaly Detection\n\nThe third step is to apply an anomaly detection algorithm to the wavelet frame. The algorithm identifies segments of the signal that deviate from the expected behavior, based on the distribution of energy across the wavelet coefficients. The detected anomalies are then localized, allowing for further investigation.\n\n## 3.4 Localization\n\nThe final step is to localize the detected anomalies. This involves identifying the specific segment of the signal that exhibits abnormal behavior, based on the wavelet coefficients. The localization process can be performed using various techniques, such as the sum of absolute differences (SAD) or the sum of squared differences (SSD).\n\n# 4. RESULTS\n\n## 4.1 Dataset\n\nThe dataset used in this study consists of 1000 time series data points, each representing a different variable. The variables include stock prices, sensor readings, and environmental measurements. The data was collected over a period of 10 days, with each day containing 100 data points.\n\n## 4.2 Anomaly Detection\n\nThe results of the anomaly detection algorithm are presented in Table 1. The table shows the number of anomalies detected, the type of anomaly, and the location of the anomaly. The table also includes a visual representation of the detected anomalies, which are shown as red circles on the time series plot.\n\n| Anomaly Type | Number of Anomalies | Type of Anomaly | Location of Anomaly |\n|--------------|-------------------|-----------------|---------------------|\n| Stock Price Anomaly | 5 | Outlier | 1 |\n| Sensor Reading Anomaly | 3 | Outlier | 2 |\n| Environmental Measurement Anomaly | 2 | Outlier | 3 |\n\n## 4.3 Localization\n\nThe localization results are presented in Figure 1. The figure shows the detected anomalies as red circles on the time series plot. The anomalies are located at different positions, indicating that the algorithm was able to identify multiple types of anomalies.\n\n# 5. CONCLUSION\n\nIn this paper, a novel approach to detecting and localizing anomalies in time series data is presented. The wavelet transform is used to decompose the data into different frequency components, allowing for the identification of anomalies by analyzing the distribution of energy across these components. The proposed method has been applied to a real-world dataset, and the results show promising performance in detecting anomalies. Future work will focus on improving the accuracy and robustness of the algorithm, as well as extending it to other types of time series data."}